 cover its left segment and **another (possibly the same) tile** to cover its right segment.  If a tile covers cells from several rows, then it must be *vertical* (its columns are fixed) and it can cover the left segment of each of those rows *or* the right segment of each of those rows, but not both.\\nConsequently a tile can be assigned a *type*:\\n* **L‑type** – it covers only left segments of the rows it occupies,\\n* **R‑type** – it covers only right segments of the rows it occupies.\\nLet $\\\\ell$ be the number of L‑type tiles and $r$ the number of R‑type tiles.\\nEvery row contributes exactly one left segment and one right segment, so we must have\\n\\\\[\\\\ell+r\\\\ge n.\\\\tag{1}\\\\]\\nNow look at the columns.  The uncovered cell in column $c$ splits the column into an *upper* and a *lower* part.  By the same reasoning a tile can be of **U‑type** (covers only upper parts) or **D‑type** (covers only lower parts).  Let $u$ and $d$ be the numbers of U‑type and D‑type tiles.  Again we obtain\\n\\\\[u+d\\\\ge n.\\\\tag{2}\\\\]\\nA tile is simultaneously of one horizontal type (L or R) and one vertical type (U or D).  Thus the total number of tiles is\\n\\\\[T=\\\\ell+r = u+d.\\\\]\\nAdding (1) and (2) gives $2T\\\\ge 2n$, i.e. $T\\\\ge n$.  This trivial bound is far from optimal.\\nTo improve it we exploit the fact that a tile cannot be both L‑type *and* R‑type, nor both U‑type *and* D‑type.  In particular, a tile that is L‑type can intersect at most $k$ rows whose uncovered cells lie **to the right** of the tile’s rightmost column.  If a tile spans $k$ consecutive rows, the $k$ uncovered cells in those rows must all lie in the same side of the tile; otherwise the tile would be forced to cross an uncovered cell, which is forbidden.\\nLet $k$ be the largest integer for which a tile can cover $k$ rows without meeting any uncovered cell.  Because the uncovered cells form a permutation, the $k$ rows must correspond to $k$ distinct columns, all of which lie either