D\\) is the length of a longest decreasing subsequence of \\(\\pi\\).  Formally,\n\\[x+z\\ge D.\\tag{1}\\]\nSimilarly the NE and SE rectangles cover the *right region*, giving\n\\[y+w\\ge D.\\tag{2}\\]\nThe NW and NE rectangles together cover the *upper region* (cells above the uncovered point in their column), whence\n\\[x+y\\ge L,\\tag{3}\\]\nand the SW and SE rectangles cover the *lower region*, giving\n\\[z+w\\ge L.\\tag{4}\\]\nAdding (1)–(4) yields\n\\[2(x+y+z+w)\\ge 2(L+D)\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad T:=x+y+z+w\\ge L+D.\\tag{5}\\]\nNow consider the cells that are immediate neighbours of the uncovered squares.  For each row \\(i\\) with \\(\\pi(i)\\neq1\\) the cell \\((i,\\pi(i)-1)\\) lies left of the uncovered point; for each row with \\(\\pi(i)\\neq n\\) the cell \\((i,\\pi(i)+1)\\) lies right of it.  There are exactly \\(n-2\\) such neighbour cells (the two rows whose uncovered squares are in column 1 or column n contribute only one neighbour each).  In any tiling each of these neighbour cells must belong to a rectangle, and by the observation made in the proof of (1)–(4) a rectangle can contain at most one neighbour belonging to a fixed increasing (resp. decreasing) subsequence.  Consequently at least \\(L+D-2\\) distinct rectangles are needed to accommodate the neighbour cells that lie on a longest increasing subsequence and on a longest decreasing subsequence.  Adding the inevitable \\(n-2\\) neighbour cells gives the stronger bound\n\\[T\\ge n+(L+D)-2.\\tag{6}\\]\nThus **any** tiling satisfies\n\\[T\\ge n+L+D-2.\\]\n\n#### 2.  From Erdős–Szekeres to a numeric bound\n\nErdős–Szekeres guarantees \\(L\\cdot D\\ge n\\); the product is fixed, the sum is minimized when \\(L=D=\\sqrt n\\).  Hence\n\\[L+D\\ge 2\\sqrt n.\\]\nInsert this into (6):\n\\[T\\ge n+2\\sqrt n-2.\\]\nFor \\(n=2025\\) we obtain the universal lower bound\n\\[T\\ge 2025+2\\cdot45-2=2113.\\]\n\n#### 3.  A permutation attaining the bound\n\nWrite \\(n=m^2\\) with \\(m=45\\).  Every index \\(i\\) can be written uniquely as\n\\[i=am+b\\quad\\text{with }0\\le a,b<m,\\]\nwhere we use 0‑based numbers for convenience.  Define the *grid permutation*\n\\[\\pi(i)=bm+a+1.\\]\nEquivalently, in the \\(m\\times m\\) array of blocks the uncovered squares lie one per block, at position \\((am+b+1,\\;bm+a+1)\\).  This permutation has both\n\\[L=m\\quad\\text{and}\\quad D=m,\\]\nbecause the points \\((am+b+1,bm+a+1)\\) form an \\(m\\times m\\) “grid’’ of increasing and decreasing chains of length \\(m\\).\n\n#### 4.  A tiling with exactly \\(n+2m-2\\) rectangles\n\nWe now describe a tiling for the above permutation.\n\n*Vertical rectangles.*  For each \\(k=1,\\dots,m-1\\) define\n\\[V_k=\\{\\,(i,j)\\mid i>k m\\;\\text{and}\\; (k-1)m<j<k m\\,\\}.\\]\nThus \\(V_k\\) consists of all cells that lie **strictly left** of the diagonal squares in rows below the horizontal line \\(i=k m\\) and whose column lies in the open interval between the two consecutive diagonal columns \\(k m\\) and \\(k m+1\\).  The sets \\(V_k\\) are pairwise disjoint because their column intervals are disjoint, and none of them contains an uncovered square (the uncovered squares have column \\(bm+a+1\\), never falling in a gap).  There are \\(m-1\\) such rectangles.\n\n*Horizontal rectangles.*  Dually, for each \\(k=1,\\dots,m-1\\) define\n\\[H_k=\\{\\,(i,j)\\mid j>k m\\;\\text{and}\\; (k-1)m<i<k m\\,\\}.\\]\nThese are the cells that lie **strictly right** of the diagonal squares in columns below the vertical line \\(j=k m\\).  Again the \\(H_k\\) are pairwise disjoint, avoid all uncovered squares, and there are \\(m-1\\) of them.\n\n*Row rectangles.*  After removing the \\(2(m-1)\\) rectangles \\(V_k, H_k\\) the board is split, row by row, into a single contiguous interval of uncovered cells: for a fixed row \\(i=am+b\\) the remaining uncovered columns are precisely\n\\[(am+b+1,\\;bm+a+1)\\quad\\text{and the columns between them}.\\]\nBecause the column gaps used for the \\(V_k\\) and \\(H_k\\) have al