teq\\{(a-1)m+1,\\dots ,am\\}$ and columns $R_{\\text{cols}}\\subseteq\\{(a-1)m+1,\\dots ,am\\}$.  Since $(r,c)$ is missing, either $r\\notin R_{\\text{rows}}$ or $c\\notin R_{\\text{cols}}$.  If $r\\notin R_{\\text{rows}}$, then none of the squares in row $r$ (except the missing one) are covered by $R$, contradicting that $R$ covers all squares of $B_{a,a}$ except $(r,c)$.  The same contradiction arises when $c\\notin R_{\\text{cols}}$.  Hence a single rectangle cannot cover $B_{a,a}\\setminus\\{(r,c)\\}$; at least two rectangles are necessary.  ∎\n\nFrom Lemma 1 there are at least $m^2-m$ tiles for the off‑diagonal blocks.  From Lemma 2 there are at least $2$ tiles for each of the $m$ diagonal blocks.  Therefore any admissible tiling uses at least\n\\[\n(m^2-m)+2m = m^2+m = n+m = 2025+45 = 2070\n\\]\ntiles.  This is a firm lower bound because the counting treats each block separately; the tiles used for distinct blocks are disjoint, so no over‑counting occurs.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A construction attaining the bound\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nWe now exhibit a permutation $\\pi$ and a tiling that uses exactly $2070$ tiles.\n\n**Choice of uncovered squares.**  For each block index $a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,m\\}$ place the uncovered square at the *upper‑left corner* of $B_{a,a}$, i.e.\n\\[\n(r_a,c_a)=\\bigl((a-1)m+1,\\;(a-1)m+1\\bigr).\n\\]\nThe rows $r_a$ are distinct and the columns $c_a$ are distinct, so the $m$ squares form a permutation matrix.  All other rows and columns contain no uncovered squares.\n\n**Tiling of off‑diagonal blocks.**  For every $a\\neq b$ place a single $m\\times m$ tile covering the whole block $B_{a,b}$.  These $m^2-m$ tiles are pairwise disjoint and do not intersect any uncovered square.\n\n**Tiling of a diagonal block.**  Consider $B_{a,a}$ with missing corner $(r_a,c_a)$.  Cover the remaining cells of the block with two rectangles:\n1. a $m\\