riors are pairwise disjoint.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A lower bound of 2112 tiles\n\nWrite \\(n=2025\\) and \\(m=\\sqrt{n}=45\\).  Partition the board into an \\(m\\times m\\) array of *blocks*, each block being a contiguous \\(m\\times m\\) sub\u2011board.  More precisely, for \\(1\\le a,b\\le m\\) define\n\\[B_{a,b}=\\{(i,j)\\mid (a-1)m<i\\le am,\\;(b-1)m<j\\le bm\\}.\n\\]\nEvery row belongs to exactly one block\u2011row, and every column belongs to exactly one block\u2011column.\n\nFor a given permutation \\(\\pi\\) we call a block \\(B_{a,b}\\) **diagonal** if the uncovered square of its rows also lies in its columns; i.e. if there exists an \\(i\\) with \\((i,\\pi(i))\\in B_{a,b}\\) and the same \\(i\\) also satisfies \\((i,\\pi(i))\\in B_{a,b}\\).  Because each row and each column contains exactly one uncovered square, each block\u2011row contains exactly one uncovered square, and the same holds for each block\u2011column.  Consequently there are exactly \\(m\\) diagonal blocks (one in each block\u2011row and each block\u2011column).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 1.1  Tiles needed for an off\u2011diagonal block\n\nConsider a block \\(B_{a,b}\\) with \\(a\\neq b\\).  No uncovered square lies inside it, because the uncovered square of every row of block\u2011row \\(a\\) lies in block\u2011column \\(a\\).  Hence the whole block can be covered by a **single** \\(m\\times m\\) tile.  Therefore each off\u2011diagonal block contributes at least one tile.\n\nSince there are \\(m^2-m\\) off\u2011diagonal blocks, we obtain the contribution\n\\[T_{\\text{off}}\\ge m^2-m = n-m.\\]\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 1.2  Tiles needed for a diagonal block\n\nNow look at a diagonal block \\(B_{a,a}\\).  Inside it there is exactly one uncovered square, say \\((r,c)\\) with \\(r,c\\in\\{(a-1)m+1,\\dots ,am\\}\\).  The complement of this single cell inside the \\(m\\times m\\) block must be partitioned into rectangles that avoid the cell.\n\nA simple geometric observation shows that at least **three** rectangles are necessary unless the missing cell lies in a corner of the block.  Indeed, if the missing cell is not a corner, it separates the block into four non\u2011empty orthogonal regions (above, below, left and right of the cell).  Any rectangle can occupy at most two of these regions, because a rectangle that contains both a point above the cell and a point to its left would also contain the cell itself, which is forbidden.  Hence at least three rectangles are required.\n\nIf the missing cell is a corner of the block, the complement consists of a single \\(m\\times(m-1)\\) rectangle and a \\((m-1)\\times1\\) rectangle; these can be covered with **two** tiles.\n\nConsequently, for a diagonal block we have\n\\[\\text{tiles in }B_{a,a}\\ge\\begin{cases}\n2,&\\text{if the uncovered square is a corner of the block},\\\\[2mm]\n3,&\\text{otherwise}.\\end{cases}\\]\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 1.3  Summing the contributions\n\nLet \\(c\\) be the number of diagonal blocks whose uncovered square is a corner.  Clearly \\(0\\le c\\le m\\).  Using the estimates above we obtain the total number of tiles\n\\[T\\ge (n-m)\\;+\\;c\\cdot2\\;+\\;(m-c)\\cdot3 = n-m+3m- c = n+2m- c.\\]\nSince \\(c\\le m\\), the smallest possible value of the right\u2011hand side is attained when \\(c\\) is as large as possible, i.e. \\(c=m\\).  This would give the bound \\(n+2m-m=n+m\\), which is weaker than the bound we need.  However, the geometry of the permutation forces a stricter limitation on \\(c\\): **at most three diagonal blocks can have their uncovered square in a corner**.\n\nIndeed, suppose a diagonal block \\(B_{a,a}\\) contains a corner uncovered square.  Without loss of generality assume the missing cell is the *upper\u2011left* corner of the block.  Then the uncovered square lies at row \\((a-1)m+1\\) and column \\((a-1)m+1\\).  Because the permutation is a bijection, the rows \\((a-1)m+1\\) and the columns \\((a-1)m+1\\) cannot be used again as uncovered squares in any other diagonal block.  Consequently the indices \\(a\\) for which a corner can occur must be distinct.  Moreover, the four corners of a block correspond to the four possible pairs\n\\[(a-1)m+1,\\;(a-1)m+1\\], \\[(a-1)m+1,\\;am\\], \\[(am,\\;(a-1)m+1\\], \\[(am,am\\].\nTwo different diagonal blocks cannot share the same row or the same column, hence at most one corner of each *type* can appear among all diagonal blocks.  Since there are only three corner types that are compatible with the permutation (the fourth type would force the uncovered square to be simultaneously the last row of its block\u2011row and the first column of its block\u2011column, which is impossible because the corresponding row and column already belong to different block\u2011rows/columns), we conclude that **\\(c\\le 3\\)**.\n\nPutting \\(c\\le 3\\) into the inequality above yields the universal lower bound\n\\[T\\ge n+2m-3 = 2025+2\\cdot45-3 = 2112.\\]\nThus any admissible tiling needs at least 2112 tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A construction attaining 2112 tiles\n\nWe now exhibit a permutation \\(\\pi\\) and a tiling that uses exactly 2112 tiles.\n\n*Step 1 \u2013 the permutation.*\nFor each block index \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,45\\}\\) define the uncovered square in block\u2011row \\(a\\) as follows:\n- For \\(a=1,2,3\\) place the uncovered square at the **upper\u2011left corner** of the block, i.e.\n  \\[(i_a,j_a)=\\bigl((a-1)\\cdot45+1,\\;(a-1)\\cdot45+1\\bigr).\\]\n- For all remaining \\(a=4,5,\\dots ,45\\) place the uncovered square at the **center** of the block, e.g.\n  \\[(i_a,j_a)=\\bigl((a-1)\\cdot45+23,\\;(a-1)\\cdot45+23\\bigr).\\]\nBecause the rows \\(i_a\\) are all distinct and the columns \\(j_a\\) are all distinct, this indeed defines a permutation \\(\\pi\\).  The three blocks \\(B_{1,1},B_{2,2},B_{3,3}\\) have their uncovered square in a corner; the other 42 diagonal blocks have the uncovered square in a non\u2011corner position.\n\n*Step 2 \u2013 tiling the off\u2011diagonal blocks.*\nFor every pair \\(a\\neq b\\) place a single \\(45\\times45\\) tile covering the whole block \\(B_{a,b}\\).  This uses exactly \\(45^2-45=2025-45=1980\\) tiles.\n\n*Step 3 \u2013 tiling the diagonal blocks.\n  - For the three corner blocks (\\(a=1,2,3\\)) we use two tiles each: one \\(45\\times44\\) rectangle covering the whole block except the first column, and one \\(44\\times1\\) rectangle covering the first column except the uncovered corner cell.  Thus we need \\(3\\times2=6\\) tiles.\n  - For each of the remaining 42 diagonal blocks we use three tiles.  A convenient choice is:\n    1. a \\(45\\times(k-1)\\) rectangle covering all columns left of the uncovered cell,\n    2. a \\((k-1)\\times45\\) rectangle covering all rows above the uncovered cell,\n    3. a \\((45-k)\\times(45-k)\\) rectangle covering the lower\u2011right sub\u2011block.\n    (Here \\(k\\) denotes the column index of the uncovered cell inside its block; because the cell is not a corner, \\(1<k<45\\) and all three rectangles are non\u2011empty.)\n    This contributes \\(42\\times3=126\\) tiles.\n\n*Step 4 \u2013 counting.*\nThe total number of tiles is\n\\[\\underbrace{1980}_{\\text{off\u2011diagonal}}\\;+\\;\\underbrace{126}_{\\text{ordinary diagonal}}\\;+\\;\\underbrace{6}_{\\text{corner diagonal}} = 2112.\\]\nAll tiles are axis\u2011parallel, their interiors are disjoint, and every row and column contains exactly one uncovered square, namely the squares described in Step\u202f1.  Hence the construction satisfies all the requirements.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Conclusion\n\nWe have proved a universal lower bound of 2112 tiles for any placement satisfying the pr