most 3, giving sum >= 85. But then they later realize that the three possible losses include the one from making them disjoint, so they should not double subtract. So they correct to sum >= 87. This is a bit confusing but they claim final bound #rectangles >= n + 2m - 3 = 2112. So the lower bound argument is a bit messy but seems plausible.\n\nWe need to check for any arithmetic errors: The final bound uses 2m - 3 = 90 - 3 = 87. So #rectangles >= 2025 + 87 = 2112. That matches the construction.\n\nPotential issues: The lower bound argument uses monotone subsequences and forced rectangles. We need to verify if the reasoning is valid: Are the forced left-side rectangles indeed distinct? They argue that if a rectangle covers left side of two rows i,i' in L, then because pi(i) < pi(i'), the rectangle would contain column pi(i) and row i, thus would contain uncovered cell (i, pi(i)). This seems correct: The rectangle covering left side of row i includes all cells (i,c) for c < pi(i). If it also covers left side of row i', it must include cell (i', pi(i)) because pi(i) < pi(i') and (i', pi(i)) is left of uncovered cell in row i'. So rectangle includes column pi(i) and row i', but does it include row i? Actually rectangle covering left side of row i must include row i and columns up to pi(i)-1. So rectangle includes row i and column pi(i). So rectangle includes (i, pi(i))? Wait, rectangle covering left side of row i includes all cells (i,c) for c < pi(i). Does it include column pi(i)? No, left side is columns 1,...,pi(i)-1. So the rectangle does not include column pi(i). However they claim rectangle must contain column pi(i) because to cover cell (i', pi(i)) (which is in left side of row i'), the rectangle must include column pi(i). Indeed, to cover (i', pi(i)), rectangle must include column pi(i). So rectangle includes column pi(i). Does it also include row i? It must include row i because it covers left side of row i, which includes row i and columns up to pi(i)-1. So rectangle includes row i and column pi(i). But does it include cell (i, pi(i))? That cell is at row i, column pi(i). Since rectangle includes row i and column pi(i), it includes that cell. So indeed rectangle would contain uncovered cell (i, pi(i)), which is forbidden. So indeed each left side must be covered by distinct rectangles. So that part is valid.\n\nSimilarly for right side of decreasing subsequence.\n\nThen they argue rows not in L∪D must have at least one cell not covered by forced rectangles, requiring extra rectangle. This is plausible: For a row k not in L∪D, either its left side is not covered by any left-side rectangle (since left side rectangles correspond to rows in L) or its right side is not covered by any right-side rectangle (since right side rectangles correspond to rows in D). Since k is not in L, its left side may not be covered, but it's possible that some left-side rectangle covers it even if the row is not in L? Actually left-side rectangles are forced for each i in L, but they could also cover left side of other rows? The argument earlier shows a left-side rectangle cannot cover left side of two rows in L, but could it cover left side of a row not in L? Possibly, but then that rectangle would contain uncovered cell of that other row? Let's examine: Suppose rectangle covers left side of row i in L and also covers left side of row k not in L. Then rectangle includes column pi(i) and row k (since left side of row k includes columns up to pi(k)-1, which includes pi(i) if pi(i) < pi(k)). But if pi(i) < pi(k), then rectangle includes column pi(i) and row k, but does it include uncovered cell (k, pi(k))? No, because column pi(i) is not pi(k). So maybe rectangle could cover left side of a row not in L. However the argument says \"Since L is increasing, pi(i) < pi(i')\". For a row k not in L, we don't have guarantee about pi(k). So it's possible that some left-side rectangle covers left side of a row not in L. But they claim that for rows not in L∪