 solution appears plausible and typical for such problems: using monotone subsequences to bound number of tiles. The argument that each row in L forces a distinct left-side rectangle is correct. Similarly for D. Then they argue that rows not in L∪D require extra rectangles. This is plausible: For each row not in L∪D, either its left side is not covered by any left-side rectangle (since those are only for rows in L) or its right side is not covered by any right-side rectangle (since those are only for rows in D). But it's possible that a left-side rectangle for some row i∈L also covers left side of other rows not in L, as we considered. However, does that happen? Let's examine: Suppose i∈L, rectangle covering left side of i includes all cells (i,c) for c<pi(i). For any other row k, the rectangle may also include cells (k,c) for c in some interval. But the rectangle is axis-aligned rectangle covering a set of rows and columns. The left side of row i is a set of cells in row i only. The rectangle covering that left side must include row i and columns up to pi(i)-1. It could also include other rows, but must be contiguous in rows. The rectangle could be of the form rows [i,i'] (or [i',i]) and columns [1, pi(i)-1] maybe. If it includes other rows, then for those rows, the rectangle covers columns [1, pi(i)-1] which are left of the uncovered cell in those rows if pi(k) > pi(i)-1. If pi(k) > pi(i)-1, then the rectangle covers a subset of the left side of row k (maybe all of it if pi(k) > pi(i)-1). But if pi(k) <= pi(i)-1, then the rectangle would include the uncovered cell (k, pi(k)), which is forbidden. So for any other row k that the rectangle includes, we must have pi(k) > pi(i)-1, i.e., pi(k) >= pi(i). Since pi(i) is the column of uncovered cell for row i, and we have pi(i) < pi(i') for i < i' in L (increasing subsequence). So for any row k > i (since rectangle includes rows i..i' maybe), pi(k) might be >= pi(i). But if pi(k) = pi(i), then the rectangle includes column